          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Gabriel García Márquez</strong><br>\nGabriel García Márquez was a Colombian novelist, short-story writer, screenwriter, and journalist, known affectionately as Gabo throughout Latin America. He is considered one of the most significant authors of the 20th century and one of the best in the Spanish language. Born on March 6, 1927, in Aracataca, Colombia, Márquez was raised by his maternal grandparents until his early teens.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Early Life and Education</strong><br>\nMárquez was a precocious child who showed an early love for writing and storytelling. His grandparents' tales would provide a rich source of material for his later work. He began his formal education at a boarding school in Barranquilla and later studied law and journalism at the National University of Colombia in Bogotá and the University of Cartagena.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Journalistic Work</strong><br>\nBefore becoming a famous writer, Márquez started his career as a journalist. He wrote for several Colombian newspapers, including El Espectador and El Heraldo. His journalistic work was distinguished by his use of literary techniques, which would become a hallmark of his fiction writing.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
