          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Literary Contributions</strong><br>\nMárquez's literary style is often termed magical realism, where he used fantastic elements in otherwise realistic situations. His most famous novel, \"One Hundred Years of Solitude,\" was published in 1967 and helped him to win the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1982. The novel has sold tens of millions of copies worldwide.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Magical Realism</strong><br>\nThe term \"magical realism\" is associated closely with Márquez. His stories often blend the magical with the mundane, creating a world where the extraordinary becomes part of everyday life. This style is exemplified in his novels and stories, where he describes the impossible and improbable with a casual and everyday tone.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Major Works</strong><br>\nBesides \"One Hundred Years of Solitude,\" Márquez wrote several other novels, including \"Love in the Time of Cholera,\" \"The Autumn of the Patriarch,\" and \"Chronicle of a Death Foretold.\" His works explore themes like time, reality, memory, and solitude, and are set against the backdrop of Latin American history and politics.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
